WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.370
We've finally gotten our first official look
at Nintendo's ninth-generation console, the

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.370
Switch.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.249
Details were light in the days after the its
official announcement.

00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:11.500
But based on first impressions, it sure seems
like the new system could be capable of going

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.540
head to head with the Xbox One and the PlayStation
4.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.140
Here's a rundown on what we know about the
Nintendo Switch so far, and how it will stack

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.689
up against the competition.

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:21.890
It's a console...and a portable!

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.220
By now, you may be wondering why Nintendo
called its new console the Switch.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.700
Well check THIS out!

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.120
"That's crazy!"

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.380
That's right, with this new console, gamers
will be able to SWITCH from playing at home

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.390
or on the go.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.420
The trailer shows more scenarios, like playing
on a park bench, on an airplane, and even

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.530
on the road.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:46.990
And when you've gotten where you're going?

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.010
Just plug that thing back into the TV.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.610
From what we can see in the trailer, the Switch
seems nowhere near as bulky as the Wii U gamepad.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:58.140
Instead, Nintendo's latest console has a sleek
and slim design that's more reminiscent of

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.140
a tablet.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.760
Ever since 1989, Nintendo has dominated the
handheld gaming market.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.640
Microsoft never really did much with its tablet-based
SmartGlass project.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.330
And Sony's portable ambitions with the PSP
and PlayStation Vita couldn't ever touch Nintendo.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:15.100
If the Switch is can truly deliver on the
promise of being a fully functional home-portable

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.860
hybrid, Nintendo will give Microsoft and Sony
a run for their money.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.850
The mighty Joy-Con

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:25.210
The Switch's detachable controllers, called
Joy-Cons, will give players tons of options.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.890
The two little doohickeys can be attached
to one controller hub, the Switch console

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.549
itself, can be used like Wii-style nunchucks,
or even as individual controllers for multiple

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:34.549
players.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:37.080
That's four different configurations for one
device.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.350
And then there's also the "Nintendo Switch
Pro Controller" that made an appearance in

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.460
the trailer.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.581
While Sony and Microsoft both offer traditional-style
wireless controllers for their consoles, they

00:01:45.581 --> 00:01:48.670
have nothing like the Joy-Con's adaptability.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.670
Mobile multiplayer

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.820
Nintendo wants to bring back local multiplayer
in a big way.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:58.560
The trailer shows more than one Switch console
being linked together, apparently wirelessly.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.510
It seems like a fair bet that the Switch will
have some form of Wi-Fi built in, too.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.040
But until Nintendo tells us more, we're only
guessing.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.750
The Switch also looks like it's built with
eSports in mind, with two teams of gamers

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.980
getting together to duke it out over a game
of Splatoon.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.590
Kick it with the stand

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:17.520
Back in the GameCube days, Nintendo decided
to stick a little handle on their flagship

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.250
console, so you could carry it around like
a lunchbox...which cost two-hundred dollars.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.030
This time around, however, the Switch looks
like it's truly equipped for gameplay anywhere,

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.030
anytime.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.030
Why?

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.030
It's got a kickstand!

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.350
This may not seem important, but small details
like this can make or break a device.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.370
If you're tired of holding the whole console,
you can set it up on a table and get comfortable.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:40.200
As far as design decisions go, that tiny piece
of plastic is actually kind of brilliant.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.310
Putting the cart before the disc

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:45.810
The rumors are true: Nintendo is ditching
discs in favor of cartridges.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.830
The Switch seems to run on small game cards,
apparently similar in size and shape to 3DS

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.090
game cards.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:56.800
Unfortunately, Nintendo confirmed that the
Switch won't play Wii U discs or 3DS game

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.800
cartridges.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.320
Still, it's possible the Switch might rely
on SD cards — just like the 3DS — for

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.010
expandable internal storage, meaning players
might be able to carry lots of games on each

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.010
card.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.440
Either way, that means lots of games on the
go.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.709
Nvidia visuals

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:14.780
On the same day that Nintendo's Switch trailer
went live, Nvidia announced that it had provided

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:19.090
the console with a custom Tegra processor,
"based on the same architecture as the world's

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.640
top-performing GeForce gaming graphics cards."

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.860
Of course, a console's performance can't be
summed up by knowing just one of the components

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.320
that's under the hood.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.840
And as of the making of this video, Nintendo
hasn't revealed any of the Switch's internal

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.069
hardware specs.

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:35.870
However, the trailer gives us a glimpse at
someone playing a game that sure does look

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:38.730
like Skyrim, both on the go and on the television.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.910
An article on Ars Technica speculates that
the Switch will switch between 720p and 1080p

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:47.209
visuals depending on whether it's on the go
or in the dock.

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:50.840
We won't know how close it'll stack up to
the PlayStation 4 or Xbox One until we know

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.120
more about its internal specs, but it seems
safe to say that it won't look like, well,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:55.620
this.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:57.620
"Hello!"

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:01.489
New exclusives and a third-party party

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:05.519
For years, Nintendo has struggled to attract
third-party gamemakers to make software for

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:06.540
its consoles.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:10.159
It's obviously way too early to know whether
or not the Switch will remedy that problem

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:11.290
in the long run.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.739
But Nintendo revealed that nearly 50 third-party
publishers and developers have committed to

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:16.989
their new console.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:21.019
We know the Switch will have new games featuring
Super Mario, The Legend of Zelda, and Mario

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:22.019
Kart.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:26.250
But having top-tier, triple-A games from the
likes of Bethesda, EA, and Capcom?

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.810
That could help the Switch get hardcore gamers
on board, while also appealing to lifelong

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.810
Nintendo fans and casual gamers alike.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.870
Waiting to flip the Switch

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.680
For all that we know — or think we know
— there's still plenty we don't.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.550
As of the making of this video, we're still
in the dark about the Switch's full tech specs,

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:47.160
its launch titles, its actual release date,
or even the console's purchase price.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.660
How long will the batteries last?

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.960
How much will it cost to replace a JoyCon
after one of them gets thrown out the window

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.830
during a vigorous session of minivan Mario
Kart?

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.650
Because that's totally going to happen.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.720
But even with the little information we do
have, it seems like Nintendo wants the Switch

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.860
to make waves.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.370
Could this be the console Nintendo meant to
make when the Wii U flopped?

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.590
Will it capture the attention and hard earned
cash of casual and hardcore game fans alike?

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.770
"Shut up and take my money!"

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:12.800
Only time will tell.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.160
But one thing's for sure: we've never seen
a console like this before.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.710
In the end, there's only one thing we can
say to Nintendo about the Switch:

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:20.710
"Good luck!"

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:21.710
Thanks for watching!

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.710
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.409
And leave us a comment to let us know what
you think about the Nintendo Switch...

